# Mobius charts in the terminal

The **Mobius metrics** and **Mobius histograms** notebooks draw with VegaLite — great
when you have a browser. This one draws the same data as crisp **text** charts using
[`term_ui`](https://hexdocs.pm/term_ui): Unicode braille line charts, block bar charts,
sparklines and gauges. They render inline here and, because they are just ANSI strings,
look identical over an SSH/IEx console on the device — somewhere VegaLite cannot reach.

Two small pieces do the work:

* `Mobius.Charts` turns a query into a chart-ready data structure (sorted histogram
  bars, one line per quantile, latest values) — all the wrangling the other notebooks
  do by hand.
* `NervesLivebook.MobiusUI` takes those structures straight to a `term_ui` widget. No
  glue in between — hand a `Mobius.Charts` result to the matching function and it draws.

Every `MobiusUI` function also takes `as: :string` to return the raw ANSI string for
`IO.puts/1`.

```elixir
alias NervesLivebook.MobiusUI

instance =
  Process.registered()
  |> Enum.find_value(fn name ->
    case Atom.to_string(name) do
      "Elixir.Mobius.Scraper." <> instance -> String.to_atom(instance)
      "Elixir.Mobius.Registry." <> instance -> String.to_atom(instance)
      "Elixir.Mobius.ReportServer." <> instance -> String.to_atom(instance)
      _ -> nil
    end
  end) || raise "No Mobius instance is running. Start one with {Mobius, metrics: [...]}."
```

## A metric over time

`Mobius.Charts.series/4` hands back `%{metric:, points: [%{timestamp:, value:}], ...}`.
`line/2` reads it directly and titles the chart with the metric name.

```elixir
"system.cpu.temperature.celsius"
|> Mobius.Charts.series(:last_value, %{}, last: {5, :minute}, mobius_instance: instance)
|> MobiusUI.line(title: "CPU temperature (°C) — last 5 min")
```

A sparkline is the same data in one line — handy inline next to a label.

```elixir
"system.cpu.temperature.celsius"
|> Mobius.Charts.series(:last_value, %{}, last: {2, :minute}, mobius_instance: instance)
|> MobiusUI.sparkline()
```

## Several series at once

`Mobius.Charts.quantiles_over_time/4` returns one line per quantile. `line/2` draws each
in its own color and adds a legend (`p50`, `p95`, `p99`).

```elixir
metric = "phoenix.endpoint.stop.duration"

case Mobius.Charts.quantiles_over_time(metric, [0.5, 0.95, 0.99], %{},
       last: {1, :hour},
       mobius_instance: instance
     ) do
  {:ok, qot} ->
    if Enum.all?(qot.lines, &(&1.points == [])) do
      Kino.Markdown.new("_No stored histogram windows for #{metric} in the last hour yet._")
    else
      MobiusUI.line(qot, title: "#{metric} quantiles — last hour")
    end

  {:error, _} ->
    Kino.Markdown.new("_#{metric} is not histogram-enabled._")
end
```

## Latest values as bars

`Mobius.Charts.latest/2` returns the most recent value for each metric. `bars/2` draws
them as a colored horizontal bar chart.

```elixir
[
  {"vm.memory.total", :last_value},
  {"vm.memory.processes", :last_value},
  {"vm.memory.binary", :last_value},
  {"vm.memory.ets", :last_value}
]
|> Mobius.Charts.latest(last: {5, :minute}, mobius_instance: instance)
|> MobiusUI.bars(title: "BEAM memory (bytes)")
```

## A distribution as a histogram

`Mobius.Charts.distribution/3` returns the histogram's bins already sorted as
`%{value:, count:}` bars — no DDSketch math at the call site. `histogram/2` draws them.

```elixir
case Mobius.Charts.distribution(metric, %{}, last: {1, :hour}, mobius_instance: instance) do
  {:ok, %{total_count: 0}} ->
    Kino.Markdown.new("_No observations for #{metric} in the last hour._")

  {:ok, dist} ->
    MobiusUI.histogram(dist, title: "#{metric} distribution")

  {:error, _} ->
    Kino.Markdown.new("_#{metric} is not histogram-enabled._")
end
```

## A single value as a gauge

```elixir
cpu =
  case Mobius.Charts.latest([{"system.cpu.utilization.percent", :last_value}],
         last: {1, :minute},
         mobius_instance: instance
       ) do
    [%{value: value} | _] -> value
    [] -> 0
  end

MobiusUI.gauge(cpu,
  min: 0,
  max: 100,
  label: "CPU utilization (%)",
  zones: [{0, :green}, {60, :yellow}, {85, :red}]
)
```

## Over the console

Everything above is an ANSI string under the hood. Pass `as: :string` and `IO.puts/1` it
and you get the same chart on a plain IEx session on the device:

```elixir
"system.cpu.temperature.celsius"
|> Mobius.Charts.series(:last_value, %{}, last: {2, :minute}, mobius_instance: instance)
|> MobiusUI.sparkline(as: :string)
|> IO.puts()
```
